course: biology
lesson: 90
title: The aquarium: the coral reef tank
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Stand in front of the reef tank and it looks like a busy town under water: clownfish tucked into waving anemones, a blue tang like Dory gliding past, a cleaner wrasse swimming into a big fish's mouth and coming out safe.
explain: The coral that looks like rocks and plants is really made of tiny animals, related to jellyfish, that build stone skeletons and share their home with tiny algae that make food from sunlight.
explain: Finding Nemo got some things right and some wrong: clownfish do live safely in stinging anemones, but in a real clownfish family, when the mother dies, the father turns into a female, and fish like Dory are not forgetful at all.
explain: Even the goldfish's «three-second memory» is a myth; scientists have trained goldfish to remember a sound or a lever for months.
fact: Coral reefs cover less than 1 per cent of the ocean floor but are home to about a quarter of all sea fish species.
fact: Clownfish live among stinging anemone tentacles without being stung, thanks to a special slime; the fish gets safety and the anemone gets scraps and cleaning.
fact: All clownfish are born male, and when the female of a group dies, the biggest male turns into a female.
fact: The blue tang has a sharp spine near its tail that it uses like a scalpel for defence.
fact: Goldfish can be trained to push levers or come to a sound and remember it for months; the «three-second memory» is a myth.
fact: Corals are tiny animals called polyps that build limestone skeletons; algae living inside them make food from sunlight and share it.
fact: When the sea gets too warm the algae leave, the coral turns white and can starve, which is called coral bleaching.
fact: Cleaner wrasse eat parasites and dead skin from bigger fish, even inside their mouths, and big fish queue at «cleaning stations» on the reef.
fact: Pufferfish swallow water fast to blow up into a prickly ball, and many also have strong poison.
fact: The stonefish is one of the most venomous fish; it lies still looking like a lumpy rock, has venomous spines on its back that can sting a person who treads on it, and swallows passing fish in a split second.
fact: Seahorses are fish that swim upright and grip seaweed with curly tails; the male carries the eggs in a pouch until the babies hatch.
fact: Sea tanks are made with special salt mixed into clean water and tested every day, and big filters clean the water like kidneys.
fact: Moray eels open and close their mouths to pump water over their gills, not to threaten, and they have a second pair of jaws in their throat that pull food in.
exercise: Why are reefs so crowded with life? | They give food and hiding places to about a quarter of sea fish | They are the warmest water on Earth, and no predators can live there | Fish are kept there by divers | They are the only salty water in the sea
exercise: Why isn't a clownfish stung by its anemone? | A special slime on its skin stops the stings | It is far too fast for the anemone to sting it | Anemones have no stings | It wears a shell like a crab
exercise: What does the anemone get from its clownfish? | Food scraps and cleaning | A ride across the reef | Nothing at all | Light from the fish's glow
exercise: What happens in a clownfish group when the female dies? | The biggest male turns into a female | All the males leave the anemone and look for a new home | The babies become the leaders | A new female swims in from far away
exercise: How does a blue tang defend itself? | With a sharp spine near its tail | By blowing itself up like a prickly ball | By squirting ink | By biting with huge teeth
exercise: How long can a goldfish remember something it has learned? | Months | Three seconds | One minute | Nothing at all
exercise: What is coral? | Tiny animals that build stone skeletons | A kind of underwater plant with roots | Coloured rocks | Dead fish bones
exercise: How do corals get food from the sun? | Tiny algae living inside them make it and share it | They eat the sunbeams that shine down through the water | Their stone skeletons soak it up | They do not need food at all
exercise: What is coral bleaching? | Coral turning white when warm water drives the algae out | Coral being painted white by divers to make the reef look clean | Coral growing white flowers | Coral cleaned by cleaner fish
exercise: Why does a cleaner wrasse swim into a big fish's mouth? | To eat parasites and dead skin | To hide from the sharks that are hunting it | To lay its eggs | To steal the big fish's food
exercise: How does a pufferfish blow up? | It swallows water very fast | It breathes in air at the surface every time | It grows extra fat in a second | It fills with sand
exercise: Why is a stonefish dangerous to people on rocky beaches? | It looks like a rock and has venomous spines | It jumps out of the water and bites people's ankles | It shoots poison darts | It wraps round your legs
exercise: Who carries a seahorse's eggs until they hatch? | The father, in a pouch | The mother, in her mouth | Nobody, they float away | Both parents, on their backs
exercise: How do seahorses stop being swept away? | They grip seaweed with their curly tails | They bury themselves deep in the sand | They swim very fast | They hold on to bigger fish with their snouts
exercise: Why does the keeper add special salt to the tanks? | Sea animals need the right saltiness to live | To make the water taste nicer for the visitors | To stop the glass cracking | To feed the fish
exercise: Why does a moray eel open and close its mouth? | To pump water over its gills | To threaten the visitors who come too close | To sing | To show its second jaws
exercise: What is special about a moray eel's throat? | It has a second pair of jaws that pull food in | It has a tiny second heart in it that pumps blood to the head | It has no throat at all | It glows in the dark
test: In Finding Nemo, Marlin the dad stays a dad after Nemo's mum dies. What would happen in a real clownfish family? | Marlin would turn into a female | He would turn into a seahorse | He would leave the anemone for good | Nothing would change
test: «goldfish forget everything in 3 seconds, so who cares if it's bored» What is right? | That is a myth; goldfish remember things for months | Right, goldfish forget everything in 3 seconds flat | Goldfish forget in 3 minutes instead | Only Dory remembers things
test: 4 clownfish, 2 pufferfish and 3 seahorses in the tank. how many legs | 0 | 36 | 9 | 18
test: A Nemo-style clownfish has 3 white stripes. You count 5 of them. How many white stripes altogether? | 15 | 8 | 10 | 3
test: «coral's just pretty rocks, right» What is right? | It is made of tiny animals that build stone skeletons | Yes, it is just coloured stone that grows | It is a kind of seaweed that grows on rocks in warm water | It is dead fish bones
test: What would happen to a reef if the sea stayed too warm for a long time? | The corals would bleach and could starve | The corals would grow faster than ever | The fish would turn white too | Nothing, warm water is always good for coral
test: «why doesn't the grouper just eat the cleaner fish, free snack innit» Which answer fits? | Both win: the big fish gets cleaned, the cleaner gets fed | Groupers cannot open their mouths wide enough to swallow it | Cleaner fish are poisonous | Groupers do not eat fish at all
test: A cartoon pufferfish blows up by gulping a big breath of air at the surface. What is closer to the truth? | In the sea it swallows water fast to blow up | It is filled up with air by its mother | It is always round | It blows up whenever it is happy and full
test: A seahorse dad's pouch holds 100 eggs, and his neighbour's holds 150. How many babies could hatch from both? | 250 | 200 | 150 | 50
test: «i trod on a rock in the sea and it stung me??» What could it have been? | A stonefish, which looks just like a rock | A piece of coral that bites | A clownfish hiding in the weed | A seahorse with a sharp tail
test: What would happen to a sea fish put in fresh water? | It would get ill, because it needs the right saltiness | It would swim faster and be much happier without the salt | It would turn into a river fish | Nothing, water is water
test: «that eel keeps opening its gob at me, wants a fight» What is right? | It is pumping water over its gills to breathe | It is about to jump out of the tank | It wants to be fed by hand | It is singing to you
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the clownfish is not harmed by the tentacles of the sea anemone. | A protective mucus upon its skin prevents the stinging | It possesses an armoured shell | It moves too swiftly for the stinging cells to be discharged | Sea anemones possess no stinging cells
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the memory of the goldfish. | Goldfish may be trained to retain learned behaviour for months | The memory of the goldfish endures for three seconds at most | Goldfish are incapable of any learning whatsoever | Goldfish retain only memories of colour
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises coral? | It is composed of small animals, polyps, which build limestone skeletons | It is a species of submarine plant | It is a mineral deposit devoid of life | It is formed from the skeletons of fish
exam: It is given that, in the seahorse, the eggs are borne in a pouch until hatching. Ascertain the parent in whose pouch they are carried. | The male | The female | Both parents equally | Neither, as the eggs are abandoned
